{"name":"Blue Carpenter Bee","hex":"#9cd0e4","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"carpenter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person skilled at carpentry, the trade of cutting and joining timber in order to construct buildings or other structures.","A senior rating in ships responsible for all the woodwork onboard; in the days of sail, a warrant officer responsible for the hull, masts, spars and boats of a ship, and whose responsibility was to sound the well to see if the ship was making water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To work as a carpenter, cutting and joining timber."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carpenter"},{"term":"bee","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Byangsi."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flying insect, of the clade Anthophila within the hymenopteran superfamily Apoidea, known for its organised societies (though only a minority have them), for collecting pollen and (in some species) producing wax and honey.","Any stinging flying insect, especially a wasp."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Xylocopa caerulea","blocks":[{"text":"Xylocopa caerulea, or the blue carpenter bee, is a species of carpenter bee."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Xylocopa_caerulea_266432866.jpg/330px-Xylocopa_caerulea_266432866.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xylocopa_caerulea"},{"title":"Carpenter bee","blocks":[{"text":"Carpenter bees are species in the genus Xylocopa of the subfamily Xylocopinae. The genus includes some 500 bees in 31 subgenera. Both the scientific name Xylocopa and common name \"carpenter bee\" derive from their nesting behavior; nearly all species burrow into hard plant material such as dead wood or bamboo. The main exceptions are species in the subgenus Proxylocopa, which dig nesting tunnels in suitable soil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Southern_Carpenter_Bee_%28Xylocopa_micans%29_%E2%99%80_%287995162522%29.jpg/330px-Southern_Carpenter_Bee_%28Xylocopa_micans%29_%E2%99%80_%287995162522%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carpenter_bee"},{"title":"Bee","blocks":[{"text":"Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species – including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees – are social insects living in colonies, whereas over 90% of bee species – including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees – are solitary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg/330px-Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Xylocopa_caerulea_266432866.jpg/500px-Xylocopa_caerulea_266432866.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Xylocopa_caerulea_266432866.jpg/1280px-Xylocopa_caerulea_266432866.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.